{"dir":"in","msg":{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggKmr556t8JWxdGnzPCr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Guard Cells: The Gatekeepers of the Leaf**\n\nEvery leaf has thousands of microscopic pores called stomata, and each one is bordered by a pair of specialized cells known as guard cells. These small, usually kidney-shaped cells control one of the most important trade-offs in plant life. They let in the carbon dioxide needed for photosynthesis while limiting the water lost through transpiration. By opening and closing the stomatal pore, guard cells help a plant balance growth against survival.\n\nHow guard cells move depends on their structure. Their walls are unevenly thickened. Microfibrils of cellulose run around each cell, and the wall facing the pore is often thicker than the outer wall. When the guard cells take up water and swell, this design makes them bow outward, which opens a gap between them. When they lose water and go slack, the pore closes. The"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":84,"cache_read_input_tokens":21613,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":84},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"93d397c8-9f87-4a7e-85ec-0e5d15e14105","uuid":"0502fc87-eb98-40b3-bd60-cfeec326a3bc","timestamp":"2026-11-03T16:39:18.365Z","request_id":"req_011CggKmqqgCuCyuX87PZm1E","aborted":true}}
